The Consumer Act of the Philippines, Republic Act 7394 of 1992, declares a policy to protect consumers' interests, promote their welfare, and set standards of conduct for business and industry. It lists five objectives:
1. protection against hazards to health and safety;
2. protection against deceptive, unfair and unconscionable sales acts and practices;
3. information and education so consumers can choose well;
4. adequate rights and means of redress;
5. involving consumer representatives in social and economic policy.

Enforcement is split by product. The Department of Health handles food, drugs, cosmetics, devices and substances. The Department of Agriculture handles agricultural products. The Department of Trade and Industry handles other consumer products. A National Consumer Affairs Council coordinates consumer programs. On the exam, match the product to the right department.
Memory aid: "Health for what you eat or apply, Agriculture for what you grow, Trade for everything else."
Sources: SRC-0068 Arts. 2, 6, 148

The Consumer Act sets minimum label contents for consumer products sold in the Philippines, whether local or imported:
- the registered trade or brand name;
- the registered trademark;
- the registered business name;
- the Philippine address of the manufacturer, importer or repacker;
- the general make or active ingredients;
- the net quantity of contents in metric units;
- the country of manufacture if imported.

The metric system is the official system for weights and measures in products and commercial transactions. Retail goods must carry a price tag, label or marking displayed to the public. A seller may not charge more than the tagged price, and must charge the same price to every buyer. In a classroom scenario where a store charges more at the counter than the shelf tag shows, the violated rule is the price tag requirement.
Memory aid: "Name, mark, maker, makeup, measure, made-in — and the price you see is the price you pay."
Sources: SRC-0068 Arts. 63, 77, 81

When a seller or manufacturer gives an express warranty, the Consumer Act requires its terms to be written in clear, understandable language. The warranty must:
- name the warrantor and the person covered;
- state which products or parts it covers;
- state what the warrantor will do if there is a defect, and at whose expense;
- state what the consumer must do to claim it;
- state the period within which the warrantor will act.

Written warranties take effect from the moment of sale, not later. Separately, manufacturers, producers and importers are liable, regardless of fault, for damages caused by defects in design, manufacture, assembly, formulas, handling or packing, and for poor information about use and hazards. A product is defective when it does not give the safety a person can rightfully expect.
Memory aid: "Warranty starts at the sale; liability needs no fault."
Sources: SRC-0068 Arts. 68, 97

A **void marriage** is treated as never valid. The Family Code lists several kinds:
- a party below eighteen, even with parental consent;
- solemnized by someone without authority, unless the couple believed in good faith that the officer had authority;
- no license, unless the case is exempt;
- bigamous or polygamous;
- made through mistake about the other party's identity.

Article 36 adds that a marriage is void if a party was psychologically incapacitated to fulfil the essential marital obligations, even if the incapacity shows only later.

A **voidable marriage** is valid until annulled. One classic cause is a party aged eighteen to below twenty-one who married without parental consent, unless that party freely lived with the spouse after turning twenty-one. Exam items often hinge on the age: under eighteen is void, eighteen to twenty without consent is voidable.
Memory aid: "Under 18 = never; 18 to 20 without consent = fixable or annullable."
Sources: SRC-0071 Arts. 35, 36, 45

Safety is not only the employer's job. Under RA 11058, Section 4(b), every worker must take part in complying with OSH standards. That means four duties:
- use the safeguards and safety devices provided;
- follow instructions meant to prevent accidents;
- observe the emergency steps;
- **report any hazard they discover to their supervisor**.

TESDA's "Practice career professionalism" unit adds the ethical side of HE work. Its required knowledge includes work values and ethics (codes of conduct and codes of ethics), company policies and standards, fundamental rights at work including **gender sensitivity**, and personal hygiene practices. A professional sets priorities, uses resources and equipment economically, seeks training, and keeps licenses and certifications current. For cooks, bakers and beauticians, that means a TESDA National Certificate. Critical occupations must be assessed and certified by TESDA.
Memory aid: "Use it, follow it, report it — and keep your certificate current."
Sources: SRC-0063 Secs. 4(b), 18; SRC-0075 Practice career professionalism

Article XIV of the 1987 Constitution sets the State's duties in education. Section 1 says the State shall protect and promote the right of all citizens to quality education at all levels and make it accessible to all. Section 2 lists concrete duties:
- keep a complete, adequate and integrated education system relevant to people's needs;
- maintain free public education in the elementary and high school levels;
- make elementary education compulsory for all children of school age, without limiting parents' natural right to raise their children;
- offer scholarships, loans and other incentives, especially for the underprivileged;
- encourage non-formal, informal and indigenous learning systems.

Section 5 guarantees academic freedom in institutions of higher learning and orders that education receive the highest budgetary priority. A frequent trap: free education covers elementary and high school, but compulsory covers elementary only.
Memory aid: "Free for both, forced for the first" — free elementary and high school, compulsory elementary.
Sources: SRC-0007 Art. XIV Secs. 1, 2, 5
